= Fitted Parameters and Specimens <sec:specimen>

All materials of MC2 were measured in the same laboratory with the same oscilloscope and probes, but on different
cores and windings. @tab:params_fit lists the main fitted parameters of all 15 materials. The measurement
coefficients vary by a factor of about ten between specimens, but they do not follow a simple function of the number
of turns or the core size: N49 (10 turns) and 3F4 (5 turns on an E core) have small values, N87 (5 turns on a large
toroid) does not. Partly this reflects a trade-off with the play rate $alpha$: a fast play rate and a small $tau_1$
produce similar rate dependence. A clean separation therefore needs the same material measured on different
specimens.

#include "tables/params_fit.typ"

= Transfer to the MC1 Specimens <sec:transfer>

The raw MC1 data contain all ten training materials measured with the same instruments (oscilloscope, voltage probe,
0.983 Ω current shunt) on the same core shapes as in MC2, but on other cores, in most cases with a different number of
turns, and processed differently (the MC1 records were resampled to 16 MHz here). Starting from the model fitted on
the MC2 recording, selected parameter groups are refitted on the MC1 recording of the same material and the others
are kept fixed (@tab:transfer_all; MC1 data restricted to the MC2 temperature range 25–70 °C): the five measurement
parameters ($tau_1, tau_2$ and the lag $eta$), the 14 amplitudes (reversible curve, play density and relaxation
weights), both, or all 32. The warm-up estimator is refitted in every case. Without any refit of the model, the MC2
parameters give 10.8 % on the MC1 recordings, against 7.1 % when all 32 are refitted. The five measurement parameters
alone recover two thirds of this gap (8.4 %), the 14 amplitudes alone a sixth (10.2 %), and both together 84 % of it
(7.7 %). The remaining 13 parameters (the play widths and rate, the relaxation rates and the feedback) add only 0.6
points. The largest measurement gains are on 3E6 (15.9 % to 10.1 %) and 77 (17.0 % to 11.0 %); on N49, N87 and 3F4,
where the MC1 specimen has the same number of turns as the MC2 one, the measurement refit gains 0.5 points or less.

#include "tables/transfer.typ"

= Smaller Models <sec:fewer>

The 32 parameters can be reduced further at a moderate cost (@tab:path, all models trained with the same loss). A
temperature-independent measurement correction (30 parameters, 7.57 %) and a common feedback gain for all operators
(29 parameters, 7.58 %) cost about the same, and together they give 7.79 ± 0.08 % with 27 parameters (ten seeds). With
the feedback gain, the cubic coefficient and the slope of the play density also independent of temperature, 24
parameters reach 7.81 %, about the accuracy of the GRU with a fourteenth of its parameters. The loss between 32 and 27
parameters is concentrated on the materials with a measurement lag: FEC014 (9.84 % vs 7.91 %, ten seeds each) accounts
for more than a quarter, 3C92, N87 and 3C95 (0.7–1.0 percentage points each) for more than another third. With a
common feedback gain, the fit drives the lag pole of FEC014 from its bound to $eta = 0$ in all ten seeds. Without the
lag, the passive model loses 0.13 percentage points, and an unconstrained model without it is as accurate as the classical
circuit only because it forms the lag from non-passive relaxations.

